WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.389
Well hello everyone, I'm so excited to be
here.

00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:07.720
I'm Jack Mustard, I'm at the Department of
Earth, Environmental and Planetary Sciences

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:14.040
at Brown University, and I'm the co-director of the Earth 4-D Program at CIFAR, and while I live in Rhode Island

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.669
I also went to school at UBC, and did a summer mining coal in Hinton, Alberta.

00:00:20.669 --> 00:00:22.900
But let's move on to what the program is tonight.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.859
So, to explore is so human.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:28.619
That's one of the things we do every day,
is we want to know what's around the corner.

00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:32.500
And certainly for the first explorers, there
was this drive - this human drive.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.170
That's what Canada's built on - explorers.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.870
The First Nations people who came across the
Poles were explorers.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.539
And then the Europeans looking for that Northwest
Passage.

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:41.640
They were explorers.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.219
So we're all explorers.

00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:45.790
But exploration is hard.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:47.739
It's cold, it's dark....

00:00:47.739 --> 00:00:53.329
So the idea here is - one of the things we're
interested in - is there life beyond the Earth?

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:56.210
And so one of the questions we have is, is
there life on Mars?

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.870
It's one of the first places we look, David
Bowie - I quoted that for you to get you started

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:01.120
- but we don't know.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.760
We actually don't know how life got started
on this earth.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.930
And so one of the things we're interested
in, I'll answer that question for you in a

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:08.410
moment, is there life on Mars?

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:14.220
If there is life on Mars, but this is the
fictional view of Mark Watney played by our

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.520
good friend there, and he's showing there that exploration is difficult.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.210
He's alone, for so long, and exploration is
very alone.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:24.290
Which gets to our question that we have - are we alone in the universe? That's one of the

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.450
questions that drives me, very much.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:32.490
And where would you go to look for the evidence
of there having been life off this planet?

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:37.250
And it is that - that question actually does
start with the Earth itself.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:44.370
Because we want to know where we got our origins
here on this planet, and we have to go deep

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.990
in time to do that.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.640
So as we explore Mars with spacecraft, and
other things, we've learned that it's a very

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.640
Earth-like planet.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.390
It has canyons, it has polar caps it has other things that look very much like we see on Earth.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:58.790
But it's also very different.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:00.040
And that's the thing we want to know.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:06.130
One of the things that happened early on in
our exploration of the Martian system is we

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.360
found this rock on Earth which has these forms
on it that looked very much like they were

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.340
fossils, on a rock from Mars.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.769
And that got people very excited.

00:02:14.769 --> 00:02:17.069
Had we found the life that would be on Mars?

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:20.930
Turns out that all those pieces of evidence
were not pieces of evidence of life on Mars,

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.680
they were teasers.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:27.769
But it led us to say, "How do you go about
looking at a planet that's as big as Mars,

00:02:27.769 --> 00:02:30.920
and look for evidence that there may have
been life there or there is life there today."That's

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.920
kind of a difficult question.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:37.120
And we're trying to explore the boundaries
of life to get to that point.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.549
And that starts in some ways, with just defining,
what do you need to live?

00:02:42.549 --> 00:02:47.510
So think of this green box as the sweet spot
that all life needs to live.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:51.989
That life needs to have nutrients, not too
many or you'll be toxic, or not too little

00:02:51.989 --> 00:02:53.180
or you'll starve.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.140
You need to have enough energy to make that work, and you need to have enough water.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.120
So those are the boundaries that we're looking for.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.079
And we can look throughout all Earth's habitats
and dark corners to find out where that is.

00:03:04.079 --> 00:03:09.060
If we try to find out where Earth began, on
the Earth, we can look at this clock of the

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:16.999
life that we see, and we can look at the clock
of life, and we start with humans, and we

00:03:16.999 --> 00:03:21.310
go back in time, and we get to this point
where the planet is only unicellular, and

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:26.120
then we reach this gap, where we do not have
any evidence of what was going on on the surface

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.190
of Mars at this time.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.629
Sorry, surface of the Earth at this time,
I'm sorry.

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:34.659
So this is a big problem, if we want to understand
where we came from.

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:40.989
Just like, where did life get started on the
Earth, let alone other planets.

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:44.620
So there's this theory out there of how life
got started.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:48.069
And Charles Darwin put it nicely, as in the
warm little ponds.

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:50.981
Somehow, it came together, and that's really where we are

00:03:50.981 --> 00:03:53.280
in our thinking is, somehow it came together.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.760
and the idea is, that you've got to form cells.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:01.819
You've got to form some way to encapsulate
life in the form of these particular pieces.

00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:06.700
So these are some of the ideas that we all
have, that it could be in hot springs, on

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:12.660
the surface or in the ocean floor, or it could
be in these rehydrating and dehydrating pools,

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.680
which I think is probably going to be the
likely place to go.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.060
But we still don't know.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:20.450
How are we going to find evidence for that,
when it's all gone on the Earth, because of

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:21.600
plate tectonics?

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.860
Plate tectonics has completely erased every
piece of evidence from the early part of our

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:27.860
planet's history.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.950
So we have to go elsewhere.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:34.850
And the nice thing is that on the planet Mars,
50% of the surface comes from the time of

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.790
4 billion years or earlier on the planet.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:41.780
And so the idea is, how are we going to go there to do it,

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.080
and so one of the ways to do that is to send spacecraft to the surface.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.350
And it's hard to do that.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:49.310
So from the orbital perspective, we can't
get it all done, but the one way to do it

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:55.470
is to go put a landrover in a bunch of airbags
and launch that to the surface of the planet,

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:59.850
and we can see that that's a rather rocky
way to land on the surface, but once on the

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:05.030
surface, these spacecraft found immediately,
there were layers of rock, evident for us to see,

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.640
and those layers of rock were laid down
by water.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.070
That was one of the ingredients, if you think
about our sweet spot - we need energy, nutrients

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.070
and water.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:15.840
This was a key step forward, and we found
that particular piece of evidence.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.190
And one of the problems with this piece of
evidence was though, it was salty.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.000
Salty and acidic.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.290
And so that was a nice piece of finding, but
it wasn't enough.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:28.380
We need to find more pieces of evidence that
there was life on this planet.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:32.180
Exploration is difficult, and it might look
easy to drive across this landscape

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.430
but you will get stuck in your wheels up to
your axle, and as you watch this rover try

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:40.530
to get out of its hole, every time you see
this shadow pass across, that's another day.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:43.870
So that's two weeks of being stuck.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:47.900
I'm sure you've done that in the mountains,
like your car got stuck in the snow.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.840
Another way we can now go - we need bigger
rovers to do more science.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:55.120
And in this case we're dropping a rover that's
the size of a Mini Cooper car, we're using

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.790
a tether to do this.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:57.790
And it works.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.120
And we're going to do it again in August.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.930
But that's to get more capable experiments
onto the surface of the planet.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:08.120
This particular spacecraft has found that
there was a lake that was pretty habitable

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.330
in the place they were looking.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:13.890
Now they're exploring up this enormous mountain
slope to understand more about the planet itself.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:19.300
And in July of this year, we're going to send
another spacecraft to actually gather samples

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:20.300
to eventually bring back.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:24.580
Because we realize we can't learn it all just
from the remote observations, we actually

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:27.300
have to go and bring those samples home.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.140
And so they're going to go to this amazing
delta, where the different colours are representing

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:35.270
the rocks we want to bring home, and when we bring those home, you watch out,

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.210
we're maybe going to find the answer to that
question.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:41.940
But before we go, this is going to happen,
possibly, and it may mess up the whole story,

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:45.960
what this is - SpaceX is planning to send
a spaceship in the year 2024, and if they

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:50.440
do that, we hope we get our rocks back before
then because it could change the story.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:53.620
Thank you very much.

